<p><a class="cimage" href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/DSC08976.jpg"><img decoding="async"  src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/DSC08976-scaled.jpg" alt="" width="840" height="560" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-125001" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/DSC08976-scaled.jpg 2560w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/DSC08976-300x200.jpg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/DSC08976-1024x683.jpg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/DSC08976-768x512.jpg 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/DSC08976-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/DSC08976-2048x1365.jpg 2048w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>A month ago, I wondered idly whether <a shot href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/2026/09/19/do-usable-translation-gadgets-exist-an-investigation/">translation gadgets (for quick word lookup) exist</a>, and while everybody said &#8220;just use your phone, dumbo&#8221;, that&#8217;s just too sensible for me.  And way too inefficient.</p>
<p>So while I pondered what to do about it, I bought some keyboards to see whether that could be the solution.</p>
<p>Small keyboards (and the one above is as small as they come) is such a cursed category.  Either the keys are just too horrible or the range is awful, or a combination of the two.  So I wasn&#8217;t expecting the above to work (a <a href="https://www.newegg.com/p/3C6-01ZY-02ZC5?item=9SIC4GSKZA3495" data-cached-time="2026-10-15T18:04:55" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/cache-2026-10-15-web.webp">DarkWalker Wireless Bluetooth Keyboard, M4 Mini Keyboard</a>), but&#8230;  the keys don&#8217;t seem awful, really?</p>
<p>Let&#8217;s see how it works, though &#8212; I&#8217;ve paired it with my laptop, and I&#8217;m going to write the word &#8220;cheville&#8221;:</p>
<p><video loop controls muted poster="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/posterkHE0m2001.jpg"><source src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/video-yPxYmI.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></p>
<p>Yeah.  It doubles up key presses like crazy.  And this is paired with my laptop, so the distance between the keyboard and the Bluetooth receiver is just a few centimetres.</p>
<p>Trying several times, I have yet to make it through &#8220;cheville&#8221; correctly even once &#8212; in addition to doubling keystrokes, it also loses some here and there.</p>
<p>It also seems to go completely AWOL if you let it sleep after pairing?  Which may be a Linux problem for all I know.  The range isn&#8217;t awful &#8212; I get five metres easily.</p>
<p>But while it&#8217;s a nice form factor, it&#8217;s completely unusable.  So onto the next:</p>
<p><a class="cimage" href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/DSC08975.jpg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async"  src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/DSC08975-scaled.jpg" alt="" width="840" height="560" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-125002" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/DSC08975-scaled.jpg 2560w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/DSC08975-300x200.jpg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/DSC08975-1024x683.jpg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/DSC08975-768x512.jpg 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/DSC08975-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/DSC08975-2048x1365.jpg 2048w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>This is a <a href="Phistone Mini Keyboard with Triple Mode ">Phistone Mini Keyboard with Triple Mode</a>, so it&#8217;s got a 2.4GHz USB dongle in addition to the Bluetooth.  Which, in my experience, usually works better on these types of things.</p>
<p>But man, that&#8217;s one ugly keyboard&#8230;  and it works!  I&#8217;m not getting any missed keystrokes or any doubled ones.  So let&#8217;s start implementing something!</p>
<p>After typing away a bit <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/aztrans.el" data-cached-time="2026-10-15T18:05:05" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/cache-2026-10-15-web-1.webp">(the results are on Microsoft Github)</a>, I&#8217;ve now got something that seems to work.</p>
<p>Let&#8217;s see&#8230;  here&#8217;s my normal position in my reading chair, and I have the li&#8217;l ugly keyboard on the table next to me, and there&#8217;s a screen over there that normally displays the cover artwork of the album that&#8217;s playing on the stereo&#8230;  So let&#8217;s see how efficient this is:</p>
<p><video controls loop poster="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/postergpqFw4001.jpg"><source src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/video-mpnOYQ.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></p>
<p>Hey!  This actually kinda sorta has possibilities&#8230;</p>
<p>The Emacs over there is querying the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/translator/" data-cached-time="2026-10-15T18:05:18" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/cache-2026-10-15-web-2.webp">Microsoft Azure Translation API</a> behind the scenes &#8212; I tried a couple of other services first.</p>
<p><a href="https://developers.deepl.com/docs/getting-started/quickstart" data-cached-time="2026-10-15T18:05:32" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/cache-2026-10-15-web-3.webp">DeepL</a> just returns one word, for instance, which is pretty useless when looking up words (because most have several meanings):</p>
<pre style="white-space: preserve nowrap;">(deepl "cheville")
=> (:translations [(:text "dowel" :detected_source_language "FR")])
</pre>
<p>I was looking for the more normal meaning of &#8220;ankle&#8221; here&#8230;</p>
<p>Anyway, Azure API is free, and returns a list of resulting words and word classes, so that&#8217;s pretty ace:</p>
<pre style="white-space: preserve nowrap;">(aztrans "cheville")
=> (:source "cheville" :translations ((:target "ankle" :type "NOUN")
				      (:target "peg" :type "OTHER")
				      (:target "pin" :type "NOUN")))</pre>
<p>Thanks, Microsoft!  Not everything you make is a crime against humanity!</p>
<p>(I mean&#8230;  that I know of&#8230;)</p>
<p>The <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/aztrans.el" data-cached-time="2026-10-15T18:23:56" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/cache-2026-10-15-web-4.webp">Emacs package</a> works by grabbing the <tt>evdev</tt> device for the li&#8217;l keyboard, so that X doesn&#8217;t see any of the events.  And then it just pops to a new window configuration and displays the input, and then finally queries the Azure API before popping back to where you were&#8230;</p>
<p>So this works, but that keyboard really is too big to be on my table here permanently.  Does anybody know of a keyboard that&#8217;s more like that first keyboard, size wise, while working as well as this larger keyboard?  Or perhaps not that small &#8212; something halfway between these two keyboards would be ideal.  And not more keys than are needed &#8212; the small number of keys on that M4 keyboard is nice.</p>
